  "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR ports the ThreadX zonal lighting controller from the Linux simulation\nto a **physical zonal-computer target: the MXChip AZ3166** (STM32F412,\nCortex-M4). It validates the port on the board and in the full X-Verse + CARLA\nenvironment, and adds ASPICE SWE.1\u2013SWE.6 evidence.\n\nThe AZ3166 has no CAN transceiver, so CAN frames travel over the ST-LINK USB\nUART using the Lawicell **SLCAN** protocol. The unchanged X-Verse Zenoh2CAN\nbridge reaches the board through python-can's `slcan` interface. The firmware\nreuses the Linux controller's `lights_protocol.c` unchanged, so the\n`0x1F1` \u2192 `0x1F4` CAN contract is identical on both targets.\n\nCloses #30\n\n## What's included\n\n**Firmware (`ThreadX/az3166/`)**\n\n- Eclipse ThreadX `cortex_m4/gnu` at the same pinned revision as the Linux\n  build. CMake refuses a modified checkout.\n- Register-level board support:\n  - 96 MHz clock\n  - USART6 with an RX interrupt and a TX ring buffer\n  - lamp LEDs: brake = RGB red, reverse = user LED\n  - status LEDs\n  - SSD1306 OLED over I2C\n- Three ThreadX threads: control (priority 10), SLCAN ingress (11), OLED (20).\n  Bounded queues, a 1 s heartbeat timer and fault handling (lamps off, RGB\n  blue on stack overflow, CPU fault or ThreadX error).\n- SLCAN link behaviour:\n  - the board reports its current state when the channel opens\n  - lamps go off when the channel closes\n  - a `0x1F5` diagnostic heartbeat frame every second\n- Image size: about 16.5 KB flash and 16 KB RAM. `scripts/flash.sh` flashes\n  through the ST-LINK drag-and-drop drive.\n- Docs: [`az3166/README.md`](ThreadX/az3166/README.md) and the\n  [UART-CAN transport contract](ThreadX/az3166/docs/uart-can-transport.md).\n\n**Tests and evidence**\n\n- **Host unit tests:** the shared protocol and the SLCAN codec, 100% line and\n  branch coverage. `ctest --test-dir build` runs 3/3.\n- **`tests/hardware_smoke.py` on the board:** 11/11 checks, including all 256\n  status bytes (median round trip 3.5 ms) and a Zenoh round trip through the\n  unchanged bridge.\n  \u2192 [`artifacts/az3166-uart-can`](ThreadX/artifacts/az3166-uart-can/results.json)\n- **`tests/xverse_carla_live.py` in the full\n  `run_autoverse.py --enable-camera-display --vcu-zenoh` stack:** the test\n  presses Manual Control keys, the real VCU processes them, the board decides\n  the lamp state, and the CARLA vehicle's lights change. 6/6 steps matched the\n  expected state (8 / 0 / 64 / 72 / 64 / 0), with rear-camera frames.\n  \u2192 [`artifacts/az3166-xverse-carla`](ThreadX/artifacts/az3166-xverse-carla/results.json)\n\n**ASPICE evidence (`ThreadX/az3166/aspice/`)**\n\n- Requirements (Markdown), PlantUML architecture and detailed design, coding\n  guidelines with justified deviations, and unit, integration and\n  qualification test specifications.\n- `tools/generate_report.py` produces an HTML report. It covers:\n  - unit tests with gcov coverage\n  - cppcheck, an informative MISRA baseline and lizard complexity\n  - firmware size and identity checks\n  - bidirectional traceability\n- Result: 23 of 26 requirements verified, 0 open static findings, 0 trace issues.\n\n**Small supporting changes**\n\n- `ThreadX/scripts/configure_bridge.py` gains `--bus-type` and `--bitrate`.\n  The default profile output is byte-identical.\n- `ThreadX/CMakeLists.txt` registers the SLCAN unit tests in the Linux build.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (#30)\n\n- [x] **Application runs on the agreed physical target:** ThreadX firmware on\n  the MXChip AZ3166.\n- [x] **Selected interfaces and behavior are demonstrated:**\n  - SLCAN-over-UART CAN contract\n  - lamps and status LEDs\n  - OLED status\n  - heartbeat\n  - fail-safe on link close\n  - X-Verse/CARLA end to end\n- [x] **Build, flashing and test evidence are documented:** see\n  `az3166/README.md` (build, flash, connect, test), the `artifacts/` runs and\n  the ASPICE report.\n\n## How to test\n\n```bash\ncd ThreadX\ncmake -S az3166 -B build-az3166 -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=MinSizeRel \\\n  -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$PWD/az3166/cmake/arm-none-eabi.cmake\ncmake --build build-az3166 && az3166/scripts/flash.sh\npython3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r az3166/requirements.txt\n.venv/bin/python az3166/tests/hardware_smoke.py --port /dev/serial/by-id/<STLink>-if02 \\\n  --bridge-source ~/autoverse/bridges/can/can-zenoh-bridge-python/src/bridge.py \\\n  --output artifacts/runs/az3166-$(date +%s)\n```\n\nThe live X-Verse procedure is in\n[`az3166/README.md`](ThreadX/az3166/README.md#live-x-verse--carla-test).\n\n## Known limitations and open items\n\n- **CAN is simulated over UART:** no bit timing, arbitration or bus-off.\n  `slcand`/SocketCAN is documented but was not run (it needs root).\n- **Internal clock:** the core runs from the internal HSI oscillator (\u00b11%,\n  measured about 0.6% fast). Starting from the external crystal failed, and\n  finding out why needs a debugger.\n- **Open ASPICE items:**\n  - the board LEDs have not been visually inspected and recorded (SWR-004, SWR-022)\n  - the build with the input timeout enabled has not been tested (SWR-006)\n\nThe X-COM Serial2CAN bridge builds on this branch and will follow in a\nseparate PR from `feature/xcom-serial2can-bridge`.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
